## std.mem — heap allocation + byte-copy primitives
## (docs/manual/20-memory.md).
##
## `alloc(N)` returns a heap-allocated byte slice backed by the
## always-emitted `__zag_page_alloc` preamble helper
## (src/codegen/core.zig → std.heap.page_allocator). The caller MUST
## free with the `free` keyword (`defer free(buf)`), which emits the
## matching `std.heap.page_allocator.free(buf)` for slice targets —
## same allocator, so the ownership contract is unchanged from the
## pre-migration shape.
##
## v0.1 Tier-1 migration: the pre-migration `builtin_alloc` router
## row emitted `(std.heap.page_allocator.alloc(u8, N) catch
## @panic("OOM"))` inline. Retired — this real .zag body is the
## single source of truth. Callers must now `import std.mem.{alloc}`
## (the bare-name router no longer rewrites the call site).
##
## The body also charges the std.bench allocation counter
## (__zag_bench_alloc) so `Counters.snapshot()` accounts for
## `alloc(N)` buffers alongside `new T(v)` sites.

pub fun alloc(n: usize) -> []u8 {
    let p: *raw u8 = __zag_page_alloc(n);
    __zag_bench_alloc(n);
    return p[0..n];
}

## memcpy — byte-for-byte copy `dst[0..len] ← src[0..len]` (callers
## guarantee the buffers are `len` bytes). The last pure-utility
## preamble helper (`__zag_memcpy` in src/codegen/core.zig) retires
## here — an index loop is fully expressible in .zag, so the
## always-emitted helper gives way to a plain fn. The preamble
## original accepted a `[]u8` destination slice (not a many-pointer)
## because the .zag call sites pattern-match on
## `self.ptr[self.len..]`-shaped expressions — those same slice
## arguments pass straight through here (the leading `.ptr` slice
## of a many-pointer coerces to `[]u8` at the callsite zig-side).
pub fun memcpy(dst: []u8, src: []const u8, len: usize) -> void {
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < len) {
        dst[i] = src[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
